-- title:
--   Surjectivity of the tangent-germ stalk maps u₀, u₁
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, and a ring homomorphism $\iota : W(\mathbb F_{p^2}) \to W(k)$. Let $\Phi$ be a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $W(k)/pW(k)$ which is special for the reduction $\bar\jmath$ of $\iota$ (its Lie algebra is the direct sum of the $\bar\jmath$-eigenspace $\mathrm{lieZero}$ and the Frobenius-twisted eigenspace $\mathrm{lieOne}$, both invertible), of height $4$, with $\mathrm{lieZero} \subseteq \ker(\mathrm{lieVarpi})$ and with complementary degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces of its Cartier module, witnessed by $h_{c\Phi}$; let $r_\Phi : \mathbb Z_p^2 \to N(M_\Phi)$ be additive, assume a canonical $L$-map for the graded Cartier datum of $\Phi$ exists, and that for every canonical $L$ the map $r_\Phi$ carries all of $\mathbb Z_p^2$ bijectively onto the degree-$0$ $\eta$-piece of $L$. Let $B$ be a Noetherian $\mathbb Z_p$-algebra with $p$ nilpotent, $\psi : W(k) \to B$ a ring homomorphism, and $t = (X,n,\rho)$ a rigidified triple over $B$ which is admissible for $(\iota,\psi)$: $X$ is special for $\psi\circ\iota$, of height $4$, and $\rho$ is an isogeny of height $4n$ from $\bar\Phi$ to $\bar X$. For $i = 0,1$ let $N_i$ assign to each prime $x$ of $B$ a $\mathbb Z_p$-submodule of $\mathbb Q_p^2$, characterised by: $v \in N_i(x)$ if and only if there are $f \notin x$, complementarity data $h_c$, $h_{cb}$, $h_{c\Phi f}$ over $B_f$, a canonical $L$-map $L$ for the graded Cartier datum of $X$ over $B_f$, and a section $z$ with $\mathrm{IsEtaSection}\ \dots\ i\ z\ v$, i.e. $z$ lies in the $i$-th $\eta$-piece of $L$ and the reduction of $\varpi^i z$ to the Cartier datum of $\bar X_f$ stands in the lattice relation with $p^i v$ via the rigidifying map built from $r_\Phi$. Assume each $N_i(x)$ is a full lattice (finitely generated, spanning $\mathbb Q_p^2$), that $N_0(x) \le N_1(x)$ and $p\,N_1(x) \subseteq N_0(x)$. Let $\Pi_0$, $\Pi_1$ be $B$-linear maps between the submodules $\mathrm{lieZero}$ and $\mathrm{lieOne}$ of $\mathrm{Lie}\,X$ in the two directions, each given on underlying elements by $\mathrm{lieVarpi}$. Finally, for each prime $x$ let $u_i(x) : B_x \otimes_{\mathbb Z_p} N_i(x) \to (\mathrm{lieZero}\text{ resp. }\mathrm{lieOne})_x$ be $B_x$-linear, subject to the germ clauses: for every $v \in N_i(x)$ and every presentation $(f,L,z)$ of $v$ as above, there are $m$ in the Cartier module of $X$ over $B_f$, a section $s$ of the $i$-th Lie piece and $b \notin x$ such that the class of $m$ modulo $V M$ equals the image of $z$ under the map $u$ attached to $L$, that $u_i(x)(1 \otimes v) = s/b$ in the stalk, and that each coordinate of $s$ maps in $B_x$ to $b$ times the image of the corresponding coordinate of $\mathrm{tangent}(m)$. The conclusion is that $u_0(x)$ and $u_1(x)$ are surjective for every prime $x$ of $B$.
--
--   This is the stalkwise surjectivity of the tangent map attached to a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module, in the form used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: over a small enough neighbourhood of each prime, the localised Lie pieces of $X$ are generated by tangent germs of Cartier-module elements coming from the $\eta$-pieces, whose rigidified coordinates constitute the lattices $N_0(x) \subseteq N_1(x)$. It feeds the construction of the stalk-level tangent-germ data for the rigidified triple $t$, from which the associated lattice datum is read off.
